(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-11-06"] = {"1.1":{"s":"The equation x² = −1 has no real root, so mathematicians introduced a number i with i² = −1. A complex number is written a + ib, with real numbers a and b. We call a the real part and b the imaginary part. Real numbers are complex numbers with b = 0. Two complex numbers are equal when their real parts are equal and their imaginary parts are equal.","w":["The imaginary part of a + ib is b, not ib."]},"1.2":{"s":"Complex numbers add, subtract and multiply like algebraic expressions, with i² replaced by −1. Powers of i repeat in a cycle of four: i, −1, −i, 1. To divide, multiply the top and the bottom by the conjugate of the denominator, which makes the denominator real. The conjugate of a + ib is a − ib.","f":[{"x":"i^(4m + r) = i^r","n":"Powers of i repeat every four steps: i^4 = 1, so only the remainder r of the power divided by 4 matters.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 1.2, p.4"},{"x":"1/(a + i*b) = (a - i*b)/(a^2 + b^2)","n":"Reciprocal of a complex number, found by multiplying with the conjugate.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 1.2, p.4"}],"w":["i² = −1, so i³ = −i and i⁴ = 1. Reduce the power with the remainder on division by 4.","In division always multiply by the conjugate of the denominator, not of the numerator."]},"1.3":{"s":"A square root of a complex number a + ib is a complex number x + iy whose square is a + ib. To find it, put (x + iy)² equal to a + ib, expand, and compare the real and the imaginary parts. This gives two equations in x and y, which are solved together. Every non-zero complex number has exactly two square roots, and they differ only in sign.","w":["There are two square roots, equal in size and opposite in sign; do not give only one."]},"1.4":{"s":"The fundamental theorem of algebra says that every polynomial equation of degree n with complex coefficients has exactly n roots in the complex numbers, counting repeated roots. So a quadratic always has two roots, a cubic has three, and so on. For a polynomial with real coefficients, the non-real roots come in conjugate pairs. This is why x² + 1 = 0 has solutions i and −i.","w":["Count repeated roots with their multiplicity: (x − 2)² = 0 has two roots, both equal to 2."]},"1.5":{"s":"A complex number a + ib is shown as the point (a, b) on the Argand plane, with the real part on the x-axis and the imaginary part on the y-axis. The modulus |z| is the distance of the point from the origin. The argument, or amplitude, is the angle that the line to the point makes with the positive x-axis. In polar form z = r(cos θ + i sin θ), with r = |z|.","f":[{"x":"|z| = sqrt(a^2 + b^2)","n":"Modulus of z = a + i*b. The amplitude is tan^-1(b/a), adjusted for the quadrant of the point.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 1.5, p.12"},{"x":"|z_1*z_2| = |z_1|*|z_2|","n":"Modulus of a product. The modulus of a quotient is |z_1|/|z_2|, z_2 not zero.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 1.5, p.12"},{"x":"|z_1 + z_2| <= |z_1| + |z_2|","n":"Triangle inequality for the modulus.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 1.5, p.12"},{"x":"arg(z_1*z_2) = arg z_1 + arg z_2","n":"The arguments of a product add; for a quotient they subtract.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 1.5, p.12"}],"w":["tan⁻¹(b/a) is not the argument by itself: check the quadrant, and add π or 2π when needed.","Arguments add in a product and subtract in a quotient, while the moduli multiply and divide."]},"1.6":{"s":"De Moivre's theorem gives powers of a complex number written in polar form: raise the modulus to the power n and multiply the argument by n. It works for every integer n. In exponential form, the number e raised to iθ stands for cos θ + i sin θ, so multiplying two complex numbers multiplies the moduli and adds the arguments, and dividing divides the moduli and subtracts the arguments.","f":[{"x":"[r*(cos(theta) + i*sin(theta))]^n = r^n*(cos(n*theta) + i*sin(n*theta))","n":"De Moivre's theorem for an integer n.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 1.6, p.16"},{"x":"z_1*z_2 = r_1*r_2*e^(i*(theta_1 + theta_2))","n":"Product of z_1 = r_1*e^(i*theta_1) and z_2 = r_2*e^(i*theta_2).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 1.6, p.16"}],"w":["Put the number in polar form first. (1 + i)⁴ is not (cos 4 + i sin 4): the modulus √2 must also be raised to the power 4.","Multiply the angle by n, and keep the angle in one unit, radians or degrees, all through."]},"1.7":{"s":"The cube roots of unity are the three numbers x with x³ = 1. One root is 1, and the other two are complex conjugates, written w and w². Together 1, w and w² have a short list of properties that make many problems easy: w³ = 1, the sum 1 + w + w² is zero, and w² is the reciprocal of w. Powers of w repeat in a cycle of three.","f":[{"x":"w = (-1 + i*sqrt(3))/2","n":"A complex cube root of unity; the three cube roots of 1 are 1, w and w^2.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 1.7, p.17"},{"x":"w^3 = 1","n":"Powers of w repeat every three steps: w^(3n) = 1, w^(3n + 1) = w, w^(3n + 2) = w^2.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 1.7, p.17"},{"x":"1 + w + w^2 = 0","n":"The sum of the three cube roots of unity is zero.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 1.7, p.17"}],"w":["Do not forget w ≠ 1: the identity 1 + w + w² = 0 is only for the complex roots.","Reduce w to a power using the remainder on division by 3."]},"1.8":{"s":"Conditions on z can be read as sets of points in the Argand plane. The modulus |z − z0| is the distance from z to the fixed point z0. So |z − z0| = r is a circle with centre z0 and radius r, and |z − z1| = |z − z2| is the straight line that is equally far from z1 and z2. To change a condition into a Cartesian equation, put z = x + iy and simplify.","w":["|z − z0| = r is a circle only for r > 0; r = 0 is the single point z0."]}};
